body:
Indeed this fixes the first case!

With regard to custom elements, I'm a bit closer to understanding what's going on. These changes improve some WPTs for me, but also regress some. Trying to nail down those regressions. One of them is:

```html
<!doctype html>
<script>
const doc = document.implementation.createHTMLDocument();
const div = doc.createElement("div");

div.setHTMLUnsafe(`<span><template shadowrootmode="open">shadow</template></span>`);

const host = div.firstElementChild;
console.log(host.shadowRoot);
console.log(host.shadowRoot?.textContent === "shadow" ? "PASS" : "FAIL");
</script>
```

Sequence is:
- `setHTMLUnsafe()` invokes the HTML fragment parser with `allowDeclarativeShadowRoots = true`.
- fragment parsing sets that flag on the temporary parser document
- root insertion target makes parsed nodes insert directly into the returned fragment
- the returned fragment’s node document is the context document
- then `<template shadowrootmode>` uses `<span>` as intendedParent.
- current spec checks intendedParent’s node document (which is the context document)
- the context document does not have allowDeclarativeShadowRoots
- the template is parsed as an ordinary template instead of a declarative shadow root

One change I was looking at for fixing this is "template" case in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#the-before-head-insertion-mode being changed from:

```diff
- Let document be intendedParent's node document.
+ If the parser was created as part of the HTML fragment parsing algorithm, let document be the parser's
+ associated Document; otherwise let document be intendedParent's node document.
```

Which does fix that case for me.


The second case I've run into (this seems trickier to me) is:

```html
<!doctype html>
<script>
let constructed = 0;

customElements.define("x-test", class extends HTMLElement {
    constructor() {
        super();
        ++constructed;
    }
});

const template = document.createElement("template");
template.innerHTML = `<x-test></x-test>`;

const element = template.content.firstElementChild;
console.log(element.constructor.name);
console.log(constructed === 0 && !(element instanceof customElements.get("x-test")) ? "PASS" : "FAIL");
</script>
```

I'm still in the process of trying to understand this one, but from what I've gathered so far, it seems something along the lines of:

- template.innerHTML invokes the HTML fragment parser with the <template> element as context.
- fragment parsing creates a synthetic html root.
- current spec creates that root with context’s custom element registry.
- top-level <x-test> is created with that synthetic root as intendedParent.
- registry lookup therefore sees the page/global registry.
- since x-test is defined, element creation queues an upgrade reaction.
- after parsing, innerHTML switches the replacement target from the <template> element to template.content.
- template.content is in the inert template contents document.
- the parsed <x-test> should remain an unupgraded HTMLElement there.
- instead, the queued upgrade reaction can run for a node that was parsed for template contents.

Edit: Maybe the rules in short for above need to be something like:
- For custom element registry lookup, use the insertion target / actual parent side.
- For DSR allow flag, use the parser’s associated document for fragment parsers.

